      "value": "Document Information\n--------------------\n\n| Field | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Document ID** | TSD-SFCC-225 |\n| **Author(s)** | Victor Crisostomo — Squad A / Technical Architect |\n| **Reviewer(s)** | [Technical Lead Name] — Technical Lead |\n| **Created** | 26 Aug 2026 |\n| **Version** | v0.4 — Draft |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Related Jira** | SFCC-225, SFCC-132 |\n\n---\n\nVersion History\n---------------\n\n| Version | Date | Summary of Change | Author |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| v0.1 | 26 Aug 2026 | Initial draft | Victor Crisostomo |\n| v0.2 | 26 Aug 2026 | Fix §2.2: removed incorrect `dw.ocapi.shop.product.afterGET` SCAPI hook reference | Victor Crisostomo |\n| v0.3 | 26 Aug 2026 | Fix §2.1, §2.2, §5.1, §8: `dw.ocapi.shop.product.modifyGETResponse` is a SCAPI hook covering both OCAPI and SCAPI; removed incorrect claim that SCAPI has no product GET hook | Victor Crisostomo |\n| v0.4 | 27 Aug 2026 | Add `badgeRootCategoryId` site preference; externalise root badge category ID from hardcoded constant to BM-configurable value; update §2.2, §5.1, §5.2, §5.3, §5.5, §8 | Victor Crisostomo |\n\n---\n\n1. Problem Statement\n--------------------\n\n### 1.1 Context\n\nThe Zimmermann storefront PDP (Product Detail Page) requires contextual product labels — \"badges\" such as Runway, Exclusive, and Campaign — to help shoppers identify special collection or editorial associations at a glance. These badges are managed by the merchandising team via category assignments in Business Manager. SFCC-225 covers the BM configuration and server-side enrichment; SFCC-132 covers the PWA UI display.\n\n### 1.2 Problem\n\nThere is no mechanism to expose badge information in the product API response for consumption by the PWA Kit storefront. Without this, the PDP cannot display badges without a separate API call or client-side category lookup — both performance-costly and architecturally undesirable. Badges exist as SFCC categories (direct children of a `badges` root category) but are not surfaced in the product API response.\n\n### 1.3 Impact if Not Solved\n\nShoppers on PDP cannot see contextual product badges, losing important editorial and collection context. Merchandisers cannot surface campaign labels to shoppers without a custom product attribute, which would require a separate data feed and engineering effort to populate and keep in sync.\n\n### 1.4 Success Criteria\n\n1. The product API response includes a `c_productBadges` field containing an ordered list of badge categories the product is assigned to.\n2. Badges are ordered by the `badgePriorityOrder` site preference, editable by merchandisers in Business Manager without a code deployment.\n3. The PWA PDP renders badge pill links above the product name for both mobile and desktop layouts, linking to each badge's category PLP.\n4. Variant products correctly inherit badge assignments from their master product.\n5. Products with no badge assignments return `c_productBadges: []`; the PWA renders no badges and the page is otherwise unaffected.\n\n---\n\n2. Constraints & Assumptions\n----------------------------\n\n### 2.1 Constraints\n\n| Constraint | Detail |\n| --- | --- |\n| Platform | SFCC B2C Commerce + PWA Kit on MRT. No alternative platform is in scope. |\n| Badge management | Badges are SFCC categories — direct children of a root category (ID configurable via `badgeRootCategoryId` site preference, defaults to `badges`) with `showInMenu: false`. No new product custom attribute is permitted. |\n| Storefront scope | Solution must work across all storefronts (AU, UK, US Dom, US Intl, EU, ME). |\n| Hook target | `dw.ocapi.shop.product.modifyGETResponse` — a SCAPI hook that covers both SCAPI and OCAPI product GET responses. |\n\n### 2.2 Assumptions\n\n| Assumption | Risk if Wrong |\n| --- | --- |\n| Badge categories are direct children of a root category whose ID is set via the `badgeRootCategoryId` site preference (defaults to `badges`). | If the preference is not set and the actual root category ID is not `badges`, the hook returns no badges. Mitigation: preference is configurable per environment in BM without a code change. |\n| `dw.ocapi.shop.product.modifyGETResponse` fires for the product GET endpoint used by the PWA Kit storefront. | Confirmed — this SCAPI hook covers both SCAPI and OCAPI product GET responses and has been verified against the SCAPI product endpoint. |\n| Variant products carry no category assignments; master product holds all category memberships. | If SFCC ever assigns categories to individual variants, the variant-check logic still works (master fallback), but variant-specific badges cannot be expressed. |\n| The `badgePriorityOrder` site preference is created per SFCC-225 before this hook is deployed. | If the preference does not exist, `getCustomPreferenceValue` returns `null`; the hook gracefully returns all matched badges in unordered iteration order. |\n| `c_productBadges` is enabled in the product endpoint configuration (Open Commerce API Settings). | If not enabled, the field is silently stripped from the response. The PWA renders no badges with no visible error. |\n\n---\n\n3. Options Analysis\n-------------------\n\n### 3.1 Options Summary\n\n| Option | Pros | Cons | Viable? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Option A** — `modifyGETResponse` hook | + No new product custom attribute + Badge assignment via existing BM category tooling + Ordering controlled by site preference + Server-side resolution, single response field for PWA | - Hook fires on every product GET (negligible overhead) - Requires API endpoint configuration to expose `c_*` field | Yes — Recommended |\n| **Option B** — `c_badgeIds` product custom attribute | + Explicit per-product control + Simple hook reads one attribute | - Requires product data feed update for every badge assignment change - Operational overhead for merchandisers - Duplicate data (badge categories already model this relationship) | No |\n| **Option C** — Client-side PWA category lookup | + No SFCC-side changes needed | - Additional API round trip on every PDP load (performance regression) - Complex async coordination of product + category fetches - Violates LCP budget (≤ 2s at p75 mobile) | No |\n\n### 3.2 Option Detail\n\n#### Option A — `modifyGETResponse` hook (Recommended)\n\nThe `dw.ocapi.shop.product.modifyGETResponse` SCAPI hook intercepts the product GET response server-side. The hook iterates the product's (or master's) category assignments, filters to direct children of the root badge category (ID read from the `badgeRootCategoryId` site preference, defaulting to `badges`), sorts by the `badgePriorityOrder` site preference, and writes `c_productBadges` to the response object. The PWA reads a single consistent field. Merchandisers manage badge assignments through the existing BM category workflow with no new tooling or data feeds required.\n\n#### Option B — `c_badgeIds` product custom attribute (Discarded)\n\nDiscarded — requires a new product custom attribute and a data pipeline to populate it for every product and every badge assignment change. The category assignment relationship already models exactly this data, so duplicating it into an attribute adds operational overhead with no benefit. Ruled out by the constraint \"no new product custom attribute.\"\n\n#### Option C — Client-side PWA category lookup (Discarded)\n\nDiscarded — introduces an additional network dependency on every PDP load, requires the PWA to coordinate two async responses (product + category), and directly conflicts with the LCP budget constraint (≤ 2s at p75 mobile) and the performance rules in AGENTS.md.\n\n---\n\n4. Recommended Option & Rationale\n---------------------------------\n\n### 4.1 Recommended Option\n\n**Option A —**`dw.ocapi.shop.product.modifyGETResponse`**hook**\n\n### 4.2 Rationale\n\nThe hook places all badge resolution logic server-side, meaning the PWA Kit reads a single field (`c_productBadges`) with no client-side branching or extra API calls. Merchandisers use existing BM category assignment tooling — no new workflow, no new data feed. Badge display order is controlled via the `badgePriorityOrder` site preference, and the root badge category ID is controlled via the `badgeRootCategoryId` site preference — giving the team full control without engineering involvement. This is consistent with how other product enrichment works in this codebase (`plugin_zim_scapi`).\n\n### 4.3 Trade-offs Accepted\n\nThe hook fires on every product GET. The overhead — two site preference reads, one master product lookup for variants, one category iterator walk — is negligible at runtime. This is accepted given the complete elimination of client-side complexity and the additional API round trip that Option C would require.\n\n---\n\n5. Technical Implementation\n---------------------------\n\n### 5.1 High-Level Sequence\n\n1. Merchandiser creates badge categories in BM under the `badges` root category. Root category has `showInMenu: false` to exclude from nav menus.\n2. Merchandiser assigns products to badge categories in BM.\n3. Merchandiser sets `badgePriorityOrder` in BM under Merchant Tools → Site Preferences → Badges (comma-delimited badge category IDs in desired display order, e.g. `runway,exclusive,campaign`). Optionally sets `badgeRootCategoryId` if the root category ID differs from the default `badges`.\n4. Shopper navigates to PDP; PWA Kit calls the SCAPI product GET endpoint.\n5. `dw.ocapi.shop.product.modifyGETResponse` hook fires.\n6. Hook resolves the category source: `product.isVariant() ? product.getMasterProduct() : product`.\n7. Hook reads `badgeRootCategoryId` site preference (defaults to `badges`). Iterates all category assignments on the category source, filtering to direct children of that root category.\n8. Hook reads `badgePriorityOrder` site preference and sorts matched categories by their position in the list (unrecognised categories sort last).\n9. Hook writes `response.c_productBadges` — an array of `{ productBadge, productBadgeUrl }` objects. No exception is thrown; errors are logged to `zimm.badges` and the field is omitted.\n10. PWA receives the product response; `product-view` override reads `product.c_productBadges` via a `useMemo` and passes the mapped badge array to `<ProductBadges>`.\n11. `ProductBadges` renders a horizontal row of pill links above the product name in both mobile and desktop slots on PDP. Each pill links to `/category/<categoryId>`.\n12. If `c_productBadges` is absent or empty, `ProductBadges` returns `null` — no visual change to the page.\n\n### 5.2 Component / API Changes\n\n| File / Endpoint | Change Required | Type |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `cartridges/plugin_zim_scapi/cartridge/scripts/hooks/shop/productModifyGETResponse.js` | New hook script: resolves badge categories, sorts by site preference, writes `c_productBadges` to response | New |\n| `cartridges/plugin_zim_scapi/cartridge/scripts/hooks.json` | Register hook: `dw.ocapi.shop.product.modifyGETResponse` → `~/cartridge/scripts/hooks/shop/productModifyGETResponse` | Modified |\n| BM Site Preferences — `badgePriorityOrder` | String preference under the `Badges` group: comma-delimited badge category IDs in display priority order | New |\n| BM Site Preferences — `badgeRootCategoryId` | String preference under the `Badges` group: root category ID whose direct children are treated as badge categories. Defaults to `badges` in code if not set. | New |\n| BM Category Configuration | Create `badges` root category with `showInMenu: false`; create child badge categories (`runway`, `exclusive`, etc.) | New (BM config) |\n| Open Commerce API Settings — product endpoint configuration | Add `c_productBadges` to the product response fields allowed list | New (BM config) |\n| `overrides/app/components/product-view/index.tsx` | TypeScript override of base `product-view`; reads `c_productBadges`, passes mapped badges to `<ProductBadges>` above product name in mobile and desktop slots | New |\n| `overrides/app/components/productBadges/index.tsx` | New `ProductBadges` component — renders horizontal pill links; exports `Badge` type | New |\n| `overrides/app/components/productBadges/index.stories.tsx` | Storybook story under `Commerce/ProductBadges` with Default, SingleBadge, MultipleBadges, Empty stories | New |\n\n### 5.3 Data Structures / Interfaces\n\n**Product Response (with hook applied):**\n\nDefaultjson{\n\"id\": \"4673TS251GVSCP1\",\n\"name\": \"Zimmermann Product Name\",\n\"c\\_productBadges\": [\n{ \"productBadge\": \"Runway\", \"productBadgeUrl\": \"/category/runway\" },\n{ \"productBadge\": \"Exclusive\", \"productBadgeUrl\": \"/category/exclusive\" }\n]\n}\n\n**Hook script —**`productModifyGETResponse.js`**:**\n\nDefaultjavascript'use strict';\nvar Site = require('dw/system/Site');\nvar Logger = require('dw/system/Logger');\nvar log = Logger.getLogger('zimm.badges');\nfunction getBadgesRootCategoryId() {\nvar preference = Site.getCurrent().getCustomPreferenceValue('badgeRootCategoryId');\nreturn !empty(preference) ? preference.trim() : 'badges';\n}\nfunction getBadgePriorityList() {\nvar preference = Site.getCurrent().getCustomPreferenceValue('badgePriorityOrder');\nif (empty(preference)) return [];\nreturn preference.split(',').map(function (id) { return id.trim().toLowerCase(); });\n}\nexports.modifyGETResponse = function (product, response) {\ntry {\nif (empty(product)) return;\nvar priorityList = getBadgePriorityList();\nvar badgeCategories = [];\n// Category assignments live on the master; a variant carries none of its own.\nvar categorySource = product.isVariant() ? product.getMasterProduct() : product;\nvar it = categorySource.getCategories().iterator();\nwhile (it.hasNext()) {\nvar category = it.next();\nif (!empty(category.parent) && category.parent.ID === getBadgesRootCategoryId()) {\nbadgeCategories.push(category);\n}\n}\nbadgeCategories.sort(function (a, b) {\nvar aIdx = priorityList.indexOf(a.ID.toLowerCase());\nvar bIdx = priorityList.indexOf(b.ID.toLowerCase());\nif (aIdx === -1) aIdx = priorityList.length;\nif (bIdx === -1) bIdx = priorityList.length;\nreturn aIdx - bIdx;\n});\n// response IS the ProductWO document directly — not response.object\nresponse.c\\_productBadges = badgeCategories.map(function (category) {\nreturn {\nproductBadge: category.displayName,\nproductBadgeUrl: '/category/' + category.ID\n};\n});\n} catch (e) {\nlog.error('modifyGETResponse badge enrichment failed for product {0} (master: {1}): {2}',\nproduct ? product.ID : 'unknown',\nproduct && product.isVariant() ? product.getMasterProduct().ID : 'n/a',\ne.message);\n}\n};\n\n`hooks.json`**registration:**\n\nDefaultjson{\n\"name\": \"dw.ocapi.shop.product.modifyGETResponse\",\n\"script\": \"~/cartridge/scripts/hooks/shop/productModifyGETResponse\"\n}\n\n**PWA**`Badge`**type and**`ProductBadges`**component:**\n\nDefaulttypescriptexport type Badge = {\nid: string\nname: string\nplpUrl: string\n}\n\n**PWA**`product-view`**badge derivation (via**`useMemo`**):**\n\nDefaulttypescriptconst badges = useMemo(() => {\nconst productBadges: Array<{productBadge: string; productBadgeUrl: string}> =\nproduct?.c\\_productBadges ?? []\nreturn productBadges.map((badge) => ({\nid: badge.productBadgeUrl.split('/').pop() ?? badge.productBadge,\nname: badge.productBadge,\nplpUrl: badge.productBadgeUrl\n}))\n}, [product?.c\\_productBadges])\n\n### 5.4 Error Handling & Edge Cases\n\n| Scenario | Expected Behaviour | Type |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Product has no badge category assignments | `c_productBadges: []`; PWA renders no badges — page unchanged | Edge case |\n| `badgePriorityOrder` not set in BM | All matched badge categories returned in unordered iteration order. No error thrown. | Edge case |\n| `badgeRootCategoryId` not set in BM | Hook falls back to `'badges'` as the root category ID. No error thrown. | Edge case |\n| Variant product GET | Hook reads `getMasterProduct()` for category assignments. Master product carries all badge category memberships. | Edge case |\n| Hook throws unexpectedly | Error logged to `zimm.badges` logger. `c_productBadges` absent from response. PWA reads `undefined`, falls back to `[]`, renders no badges. | Error |\n| `c_productBadges` not enabled in API endpoint configuration | Field silently stripped from response. Same graceful degradation as hook failure. Monitoring should alert on missing field. | Error |\n| Badge category has no `displayName` | `productBadge` is an empty string in the response. Merchandiser must always set a display name on badge categories. | Edge case |\n\n### 5.5 Configuration & Environment Notes\n\n* `badgePriorityOrder` site preference (type: String) must be created on the `SitePreferences` system object in every environment (DEV, SIT, UAT, PROD) and set to the desired comma-delimited category ID order.\n* `badgeRootCategoryId` site preference (type: String) must be created on the `SitePreferences` system object in every environment. Set to the ID of the root category whose direct children are badge categories (e.g. `badges`). If left blank, the hook defaults to `badges`. Both preferences appear in BM under Merchant Tools → Site Preferences → **Badges**.\n* The `badges` root category (or the category specified in `badgeRootCategoryId`) must be created in the SFCC catalogue in each environment before the hook is deployed.\n* Badge child categories (`runway`, `exclusive`, `campaign`, etc.) must be created under the root and products assigned to them in each environment.\n* `c_productBadges` must be enabled in the product endpoint configuration under Administration → Site Development → Open Commerce API Settings.\n* `plugin_zim_scapi` cartridge must remain in the site cartridge path. Hook registration is in `plugin_zim_scapi/cartridge/scripts/hooks.json`.\n\n---\n\n6. Testing & Validation\n-----------------------\n\n### 6.1 Test Coverage\n\n| Test Type | What is Being Tested | Pass Criteria |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | Hook returns `[]` when product has no badge category assignments | `c_productBadges` is an empty array |\n| Unit | Hook returns badges in `badgePriorityOrder` order when preference is set | Array order matches priority list |\n| Unit | Hook reads from `getMasterProduct()` when product is a variant | Badges match master product's category assignments |\n| Unit | Hook appends unrecognised badge categories after prioritised ones | Unrecognised badges sort to end of array |\n| Unit | Hook returns all matched badges in iteration order when `badgePriorityOrder` is not set | No error; array contains all matched badges |\n| Unit | Hook uses `badgeRootCategoryId` preference when set; falls back to `badges` when not set | Categories filtered correctly against configured root ID |\n| Integration | SCAPI product GET response includes `c_productBadges` for a product assigned to a badge category | Field present; value matches expected badge name and URL |\n| Integration | Variant product GET returns badges from the master product | `c_productBadges` reflects master's category assignments, not variant's (empty) |\n| Integration | Merchandiser updates `badgePriorityOrder` in BM; response reflects new order without deployment | New order returned on next product GET |\n| E2E / Playwright | @p0 — PDP renders badge pill links above product name for a product assigned to at least one badge category | Badge pills visible; links navigate to correct category PLP |\n\n### 6.2 Regression Risk\n\nThe hook appends a new field (`c_productBadges`) and does not modify any existing fields on the product response. Regression risk to existing product response consumers is low. The `product-view` override is a full TypeScript port of the base component with badges added above the product name — all original prop handling, rendering, and forwardRef behaviour is preserved. The existing PDP should be smoke-tested post-deployment to confirm no regressions in add-to-cart, swatches, quantity, or other product options. Cartridge path ordering must be verified in all environments after deployment.\n\n---\n\n7. Risks & Mitigations\n----------------------\n\n| Risk | Likelihood | Mitigation | Owner |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `c_productBadges` not enabled in API endpoint configuration — field silently absent from response | Med | Verify Open Commerce API Settings in BM before dev starts. Add integration test asserting field presence in product response. | Developer |\n| `badges` root category not created in an environment — hook iterates categories, finds no children, returns `[]` | Med | Include category creation in the deployment runbook. Validate in each environment as part of release checklist. | Developer / DevOps |\n| `badgePriorityOrder` not set — badges present but display order non-deterministic | Low | Set preference value before go-live. Merchandiser to confirm intended badge order prior to launch. | Merchandiser / Developer |\n| `badgeRootCategoryId` not set or set to wrong value in an environment — hook returns no badges | Low | Preference defaults to `badges` in code; only needs setting if the actual root category ID differs. Include in environment setup checklist. | Developer |\n| Variant product master lookup adds non-trivial overhead on high-traffic PDPs | Low | Single `getMasterProduct()` call per variant GET; SFCC caches master product objects within the request context. Benchmark on sandbox if concern arises. | Developer |\n| `product-view` TypeScript override diverges from base component in future PWA Kit upgrades | Med | Review override diff against upstream on each PWA Kit version bump. The TypeScript port is a faithful copy — minimal custom logic reduces merge surface. | Developer |\n\n---\n\n8. Decision Record\n------------------\n\n| Date | Decision Maker | Decision | Outcome / Rationale |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 26 Aug 2026 | Victor Crisostomo — Technical Architect | `modifyGETResponse` hook (Solution 2) chosen over custom product attribute (Solution 1) | No new product attribute required; badge category assignments already model the relationship in BM; merchandiser workflow unchanged |\n| 26 Aug 2026 | Victor Crisostomo — Technical Architect | Variant badge lookup uses `getMasterProduct()` | SFCC category assignments are on the master product; a variant's own category list is empty — confirmed by runtime testing |\n| 26 Aug 2026 | Victor Crisostomo — Technical Architect | `response.c_productBadges` set directly (not `response.object.c_productBadges`) | In `modifyGETResponse`, `response` IS the `ProductWO` document — confirmed by runtime error: `Unknown property 'object' for class 'com.demandware.wapi.document.shop.product.ProductWO'` |\n| 26 Aug 2026 | Victor Crisostomo — Technical Architect | `dw.ocapi.shop.product.modifyGETResponse` confirmed as the correct hook for both SCAPI and OCAPI product GET responses | Confirmed against SFCC hook documentation and verified by calling the SCAPI product endpoint on sandbox |\n| 27 Aug 2026 | Victor Crisostomo — Technical Architect | Root badge category ID externalised to `badgeRootCategoryId` site preference (defaults to `badges`) | Avoids a code deployment if the root category ID needs to differ between environments; consistent with how `badgePriorityOrder` is handled |\n| [DD MMM] | [Name, Role] | TSD approved for implementation | Approved — no changes required |\n\n### Sign-off\n\n| Role | Name | Date | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Author | Victor Crisostomo | 26 Aug 2026 | Submitted |\n| Technical Lead / Reviewer | [Name] | [DD MMM YYYY] | Pending |",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
